  "query_preview": "..An active monsoon pattern continues across Arizona\nas monsoonal moisture lingers across the state. Confidence\ncontinues to increase today in an even more active period of rain\nand thunderstorms developing from late tonight through early\nThursday morning and a Flash Flood Watch has been issued for all\nof northern Arizona.\n\nFor today, we expect scattered showers and thunderstorms to\ndevelop with prime daytime heating. Most of this activity looks to\nfavor the Mogollon Rim and areas north/east. Storm motion around\n15 mph should limit the flash flood threat, though isolated heavy\nrain leading to flooding cannot be ruled out. Thunderstorm \nactivity looks to increase substantially after midnight tonight \nand through the day on Wednesday as increasing moisture interacts \nwith a shortwave feature that's expected to move across northern \nMexico and through Arizona over the next 48 hours. This pattern is\nfavorable for multiple rounds of shower and thunderstorm \nactivity, and that's very consistent with what the latest CAM \n(convection allowing) model guidance suggests. The first round \nwill likely develop after midnight tonight and move north through \nthe morning hours, followed by multiple additional rounds on \nWednesday. Storms will be capable of producing heavy rain, and the\nrepeated nature of activity will increase the threat of flash \nflooding, thus a Flash Flood Watch has been issued for all of \nApache, Navajo, Coconino, Yavapai and northern Gila counties. The \npotential for the highest rainfall amounts are in-line with WPC's \n\"Moderate\" risk of excessive rainfall across eastern Yavapai and \nGila counties. The areas that see the more persistent bands of \nrainfall could see amounts of 1-3+ inches over the next 48 hours. \nThe persistent trough across the Pac. Northwest is adding some \nwind shear to the environment as well. Though flash flooding will \nbe the primary threat, there will be secondary threat of isolated \nsevere thunderstorms that could produce large hail and damaging \nwind. \n\nStorm coverage will decrease Thursday, but we still expect to see\nat least scattered thunderstorm coverage. By Friday, the\naforementioned trough begins to progress through the western U.S.\nand will begin to spread drier air across the state. Expect a\ndownturn in activity over the weekend with mostly isolated\nshowers/storms. \n\nForecast confidence decreases dramatically early next week as\nadditional troughs move into the southwest and the guidance is\nsplit on potential interactions with additional moisture.",
